---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/genetics-inheritance-patterns">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Biology lesson. The foundational lesson <strong>DNA and
    heredity</strong> is the real prerequisite — you need to know what genes are made of and
    where they live — and <strong>cell structure</strong> helps for the chromosome packing.
    Everything about how those genes play out in offspring is built here.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>use the vocabulary precisely: gene, allele, locus, genotype, phenotype, dominant, recessive;</li>
    <li>say what mitosis and meiosis each deliver, and how meiosis shuffles genes two ways;</li>
    <li>work monohybrid crosses and Punnett squares, including the test cross;</li>
    <li>read a pedigree and reason about carriers, sex linkage and probability;</li>
    <li>explain where the simple 3:1 world ends — codominance, linkage and polygenic traits.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Mendel's insight — that traits pass as <strong>discrete particles, blended never</strong> — was
    30 years ahead of its evidence and 90 ahead of its mechanism. He crossed pure-breeding pea
    lines, counted 29,000 plants across seven traits, and found the same astonishing pattern every
    time: the first generation showed only one form of each trait, the second re-showed both in a
    stubborn 3:1 ratio. Blending inheritance, the prevailing idea of his day, could never explain
    why a hidden trait skipped a generation and reappeared intact. This lesson is the working
    arithmetic of those particles: what genes and alleles physically are, the cell divisions that
    shuffle them, Punnett squares, and the inheritance patterns that pedigrees reveal. The DNA
    lesson told you what genes are made of; this one tells you how they play.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Punnett square in motion — four boxes, one ratio, a century of insight" widget="equationStep" config={punnettCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels as a ladder. The first builds
    the 3:1 ratio from two hybrid parents; the second separates the letters (genotype) from the
    look (phenotype); the third shows the test cross that exposes a hidden letter; the fourth
    applies it all to human families. Keep the square in mind — every cross later in the lesson
    is one of these four boxes.
  </p>

  <h2>Chromosomes, genes and proteins</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>gene</strong> is a length of DNA that codes for a protein (or a functional RNA). A
    <strong>chromosome</strong> is one enormously long DNA molecule wound around histone proteins —
    a storage solution that packs roughly 2 metres of DNA into each human cell. Humans carry 23
    pairs, 46 chromosomes total: one of each pair from the mother, one from the father. Because the
    pairs carry the <em>same genes in the same order</em>, they are called
    <strong>homologous pairs</strong>, and an organism with pairs is <strong>diploid</strong>. A
    gene's location on a chromosome is its <strong>locus</strong>; alternative versions of the same
    gene occupying the same locus are <strong>alleles</strong>. The allele sequence determines the
    protein's amino acid sequence; the protein does the work; the trait follows. Sickle-cell
    anaemia is the canonical chain: one base change in the haemoglobin gene swaps one amino acid,
    the protein clumps under low oxygen, red cells deform, and a systemic disease follows from a
    single letter of DNA.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the two alleles at a locus are identical, the individual is <strong>homozygous</strong> for
    that gene; if different, <strong>heterozygous</strong>. When heterozygous, usually only one
    allele shows in the phenotype — the <strong>dominant</strong> one (written as a capital, T)
    masks the <strong>recessive</strong> one (lowercase, t). Dominance is a statement about
    expression, not strength or value: the dominant allele often simply codes for a working
    protein, while the recessive one codes for a broken version, and one working copy suffices.
    The vocabulary is worth keeping straight, because examiners and geneticists both lean on it:
    <em>gene</em> for the locus, <em>allele</em> for the variant, <em>genotype</em> for the letters,
    <em>phenotype</em> for the observable trait. Two tall plants — TT and Tt — share a phenotype
    while carrying different genotypes, and only breeding tests can tell them apart. That hidden
    layer, a phenotype concealing its genotype, is precisely what made inheritance look so
    mysterious before Mendel counted.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each term with what it names" widget="match" config={gnVocabMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "dominant alleles are stronger and gradually take over a
  population." Dominance describes expression in a heterozygote, not competitive strength or
  frequency: polydactyly is dominant yet rare, while recessive cystic fibrosis persists hidden
  in carriers for generations. What spreads in a population is decided by selection and
  chance, not by which allele is written as a capital letter.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> genes are lengths of DNA on homologous chromosome pairs, and
    mitosis copies the whole set exactly — growth's guarantee. But inheritance needs the set
    halved as well as copied, and that is a different machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Mitosis: copying the set</h2>
  <p>
    Body cells need exact copies, and <strong>mitosis</strong> plus the cell cycle delivers them.
    Before division, <em>interphase</em> duplicates every chromosome, so each one becomes two
    identical <strong>sister chromatids</strong> joined at a centromere. In mitosis proper these
    line up on the cell's equator, spindle fibres pull one chromatid of each to opposite poles,
    and the cell pinches into two genetically identical diploid daughters. Growth, wound healing
    and asexual reproduction all run on this — millions of your cells divide every
    second, and every division is a fidelity test. The machinery is impressively accurate
    (mistakes in around 1 in 10¹⁰ bases after proofreading and repair), but not perfect, and
    uncorrected copies of a damaged gene are one route by which cancers begin. The stages have
    tidy names worth knowing in order: prophase (chromosomes condense, spindle forms), metaphase
    (chromatids align at the equator), anaphase (sister chromatids separate), telophase (two
    nuclei re-form) — remembered by the acronym PMAT. The choreography matters because it is the
    physical guarantee of the genetic result: equal, exact shares.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="stack the mitosis choreography" widget="order" config={gnPMATOrder} />

  <h2>Meiosis: halving the set</h2>
  <p>
    Sex requires a problem-solver: if a sperm (23 chromosomes) met an egg (23) that were both
    diploid, the child would have 92. <strong>Meiosis</strong> is the special two-stage division
    that halves the number, producing four haploid gametes. The critical difference from mitosis
    happens in <strong>meiosis I</strong>: whole homologous chromosomes — not chromatids — are
    pulled to opposite poles, so the chromosome number drops from diploid to haploid. Meiosis II
    then separates the sister chromatids, mitosis-style. Two layers of shuffling make every gamete
    genetically unique: in <strong>crossing over</strong>, homologous chromosomes physically swap
    segments while paired up (one to three crossovers per chromosome on average); in
    <strong>independent assortment</strong>, each homologous pair lines up at the equator
    independently, so human cells can produce 2²³ ≈ 8.4 million genetically different gametes even
    before crossing over. Fertilisation then multiplies that by another 8.4 million — which is why
    no two people (except identical twins) share a genome.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="true or false — the two shuffles" widget="judge" config={gnShuffleJudge} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> meiosis is just "mitosis twice". The two divisions do
    fundamentally different jobs. Meiosis I halves the chromosome number (the reduction division);
    meiosis II merely separates copies, like mitosis. Skipping the reduction would produce
    inviable embryos with double the chromosome count — the reason aneuploidies such as trisomy 21,
    from a failed meiotic separation called non-disjunction, are such a distinctive class of
    genetic disease.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> meiosis halves the set and shuffles it — crossing over and
    independent assortment make every gamete genetically unique. Mendel's ratios are simply what
    happens when two such shuffled lots are matched up, and we can now do that box by box.
  </p>

  <h2>Monohybrid inheritance: working the squares</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>monohybrid cross</strong> tracks one gene. Take two heterozygous tall peas, Tt × Tt.
    Each parent makes equal numbers of T and t gametes, so a Punnett square gives TT, Tt, Tt, tt —
    a <strong>genotype ratio of 1:2:1</strong> and a <strong>phenotype ratio of 3:1</strong>
    (three tall, one short). Mendel counted 787 tall and 277 short in one such experiment: 2.84:1,
    superbly close. Two other crosses everyone must know: TT × tt gives 100% Tt offspring (all
    tall, all carriers of hidden shortness), and the <strong>test cross</strong> Tt × tt gives a
    1:1 ratio — the trick for discovering whether a dominant-looking organism is TT or Tt, because
    any short offspring immediately expose a hidden t.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "after three unaffected children, the fourth is owed the
  disease." Every pregnancy is an independent draw: the 1-in-4 chance for a child of two
  carriers resets each time, which is why one family can have four affected children and
  another four unaffected ones. Coins have no memory, and neither do gametes.</p>
  <p>
    Humans apply this constantly. <strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong> is recessive: two carrier
    parents (Ff × Ff) have a 1-in-4 chance per child of ff disease — and, crucially, that
    probability resets with every pregnancy, so a family can have four affected children or four
    unaffected ones. A genetic counsellor works backwards from the pedigree: an unaffected
    sibling of a CF patient has a ⅔ chance of being a carrier (⅓ of unaffected offspring of Ff ×
    Ff are FF, ⅔ are Ff), and combined with population carrier frequencies that becomes a
    concrete risk figure for their own children. <strong>Sickle-cell anaemia</strong> works the
    same way, but shows why recessive alleles persist: carriers (heterozygotes) are resistant to
    malaria, so in malarial regions the "bad" allele is actively maintained — heterozygote
    advantage. <strong>Polydactyly</strong> (extra fingers) is dominant, appearing in every
    generation of a family, yet remains rare because carriers rarely passed it on historically.
    Family tree (pedigree) analysis reads these patterns backwards: a trait skipping generations
    signals recessive; affected fathers passing it to all daughters but no sons signals a gene on
    the X chromosome.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file the pedigree clues by inheritance pattern" widget="sort-buckets" config={gnPedigreeBuckets} />

  <h2>Beyond the simple cases</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Incomplete dominance and codominance.</strong> Red × white snapdragons give pink
    offspring — the heterozygote is a genuine blend, and the 1:2:1 genotype ratio becomes visible
    in the phenotype. In <strong>codominance</strong> both alleles express fully at once: AB blood
    type carries both A and B antigens on every red cell. ABO is the classic exam system — the Iᴮ
    allele is codominant with Iᴬ but both dominant over i (type O), producing six genotypes and
    four phenotypes, and the reason O-negative donors are universal but can receive only from
    other O individuals.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="fill the blanks — beyond dominant and recessive" widget="cloze" config={gnCoCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>Sex linkage.</strong> The X chromosome is large and gene-rich; the Y is small. Genes on
    X have no counterpart on Y, so males (XY) express whatever their single X carries — including
    recessive alleles. A colour-blind father (X^cY) passes X^c only to daughters, who become
    carriers; a carrier mother (X^C X^c) has a 50% chance of colour-blind sons. Haemophilia runs
    the same path — Queen Victoria was a carrier and her descendants carried haemophilia through
    the royal houses of Europe. Females are affected only with two copies, which is why sex-linked
    recessive conditions hit males far more often: about 1 in 12 men is red–green colour blind,
    versus about 1 in 200 women.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a colour-blind father passes colour blindness to his sons."
  Fathers pass their X only to daughters: a colour-blind father's sons receive his Y and a
  fresh X from their mother, so they escape unless she is a carrier. It is his daughters who
  must at least carry the allele — the criss-cross pattern that makes sex linkage easy to spot
  in a pedigree.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="the criss-cross — drag from sons to daughters" widget="compare" config={gnCrissCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>Two genes at once.</strong> Mendel's dihybrid crosses (round/wrinkled × yellow/green
    seeds) produced the 9:3:3:1 ratio — but only because pea seed colour and shape genes sit on
    <em>different</em> chromosomes, assorting independently. Genes on the same chromosome are
    <strong>linked</strong> and tend to be inherited together, breaking apart only when crossing
    over falls between them; the further apart two genes are, the more often recombination
    separates them. Measuring recombination frequencies between linked genes — linkage analysis —
    became the first method of mapping gene positions, decades before anyone could read DNA. In
    fruit flies, Sturtevant's 1913 linkage map — built entirely from counted offspring — placed six
    genes in order on a chromosome with correct relative spacing, a result so striking that it
    convinced the remaining sceptics that chromosomes really were the carriers of heredity.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="map the pair — run the linkage decision" widget="branch" config={gnLinkageBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>Polygenic reality.</strong> Height, skin colour, blood pressure, diabetes risk: most
    human traits are shaped by thousands of genes plus environment. Each gene contributes a little,
    so populations form smooth bell curves instead of 3:1 ratios, and environment sets the final
    value — identical twins reared apart still differ in height by a few centimetres. Simple
    Mendelian cases are the exceptions that taught the rules; the ratio arithmetic remains the
    foundation on which the messy multigene reality is understood.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the faulty line — the polygenic answer" widget="spot-error" config={gnPolySpot} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Two carrier parents (Ff × Ff) have three unaffected children. What is the chance the fourth has cystic fibrosis?</li>
    <li>How does meiosis I differ from mitosis, and why does the difference matter?</li>
    <li>A tall pea plant of unknown genotype is crossed with a short one (tt); half the offspring are short. What was the tall parent?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Exactly 1 in 4 — probabilities reset with every pregnancy;
    the earlier children change nothing. (2) In meiosis I whole homologous chromosomes separate,
    halving the chromosome number; mitosis separates sister chromatids and keeps it. Without the
    reduction, fertilisation would double the chromosome count every generation. (3) Tt — the
    1:1 ratio of tall to short offspring exposes the hidden recessive allele; a TT parent could
    produce no short offspring at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Genes come in alleles at matching loci on homologous pairs; genotype is the letters, phenotype the trait.</li>
    <li>Dominant masks recessive in a heterozygote — a statement about expression, not strength or frequency.</li>
    <li>Mitosis copies the set exactly; meiosis halves it and shuffles it two ways.</li>
    <li>Tt × Tt gives a 1:2:1 genotype ratio and a 3:1 phenotype ratio; the test cross Tt × tt gives 1:1.</li>
    <li>Recessive conditions skip generations through unaffected carriers; each pregnancy is an independent draw.</li>
    <li>X-linked recessives hit males far more often, because males carry only one X.</li>
    <li>Codominance, linkage and polygenic inheritance extend — not break — Mendel's rules.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling intermediate lesson <strong>reproduction in humans</strong> puts meiosis to work
    making gametes and follows the cycle these probabilities govern; beyond it, advanced work
    extends pedigrees and linkage analysis into whole-genome territory.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Probability is the grammar underneath every square. The product rule (both events: multiply)
    handles sequential births — two carrier parents having two unaffected children is ¾ × ¾ = 9/16,
    not ¾. The sum rule (either event: add) handles alternative outcomes. The 3:1 ratio is really
    two coin-flips: each gamete has a ½ chance of carrying t, so the chance of tt is ½ × ½ = ¼.
    Real data wobble around ratios because samples are finite — chi-square testing quantifies
    whether a deviation matters, and Mendel's famously clean numbers have fuelled a century-long
    (unresolved) debate about whether he stopped counting when the data looked good.
  </p>
  <p>
    The exceptions keep teaching. <strong>Epistasis</strong> — one gene masking another — modifies
    classic ratios (the 9:3:4 labrador-coat ratio is a standard case: the "e/e" genotype erases the
    B locus's black/brown choice entirely). <strong>Sex-limited and sex-influenced traits</strong>
    show that dominance itself can depend on hormonal context. <strong>Genomic imprinting</strong>
    means some genes are expressed only when inherited from one parent — a direct violation of
    Mendel's symmetry that underlies Prader–Willi and Angelman syndromes. And mitochondrial DNA
    (16,569 bases, 37 genes) is inherited from the mother alone, creating an entirely separate
    maternal inheritance line used to trace human migrations. The Punnett square, built for one
    gene on one autosome, is the atom from which all of this complexity is assembled — which is
    exactly why it remains, 160 years on, the first tool every geneticist learns.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mendel, Versuche über Pflanzenhybriden (1866); OpenStax, Biology 2e (Patterns of Inheritance); Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus, unit 17. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
